<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claros</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Claros was an ancient sanctuary and oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, in the territory of Colophon near modern Ahmetbeyli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Its oracle, delivered through a priest who drank from a sacred spring, was among the most famous of the Greek world and flourished especially in the Roman imperial period. The temple ruins were excavated in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claros.jpg" alt="Claros" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikater · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Klaros01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Claros lay on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, twelve kilometres south of Colophon, one of the twelve cities of the Ionian League, and two kilometres north of the coastal city of Notion. Apollo was honoured there as Apollo Clarius, and the Claria games were held in his honour every fifth year. A sacred cave near the temple, important in Hellenistic and Roman times, points to an early cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Cybele</a>. In the 1st century BCE, as the hinterland was deforested, the site was buried in silt from the small river beside it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the founding myth the seer Manto, daughter of Teiresias, was sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> from Delphi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> with captive Thebans; the Cretan Rhacius married her and let them found Claros, and their heir was the seer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mopsus">Mopsus</a>, who defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calchas">Calchas</a> in a contest of divination. Excavation has found a religious area around a spring of fresh water from the 9th century BCE, a round altar of the later 7th century and, by the mid-6th century, a marble temple of Apollo and a precinct of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>.</p><p>A new temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> was begun in the 3rd century BCE, with a dark crypt-like adyton from which the oracle spoke. By legend the oracle advised the people of Smyrna to move to Mount Pagos. Pliny the Elder wrote that drinking from the pool in the Clarian cave gave the power of prophecy but shortened life, and the oracle was said to have hinted at an early doom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a>, who visited in 18 CE. Fifty tons of marble columns from Proconnesos, bound for Claros, were recovered from a late Hellenistic wreck off Kizilburun found in 1993.</p><p>T. Macridy uncovered the monumental entrance in 1905 and returned with Charles Picard in 1913. Louis Robert excavated from 1950 to 1961, revealing the Doric Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and Roman monuments, and the Sacred Way was cleared under J. de La Genière in 1988.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins lie north of Ahmetbeyli in the Menderes district of İzmir Province. The adyton's narrow vaulted corridors survive, with fragments of a colossal seated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leto">Leto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>, over seven metres high. Its inscriptions form the largest surviving assembly of Greek inscriptions, and rows of iron rings show how a hundred victims could be sacrificed at once.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Claros” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The oracle of Apollo at <b>Claros</b> lay in its territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/colophon">Colophon</a></li></ul></section>
